{"format":"hologram.registry.artifact/v1","id":"mcr.microsoft.com/azurelinux/busybox","fetched":"2026-09-25T12:12:07.450Z","source":{"name":"Microsoft Artifact Registry","api":"https://mcr.microsoft.com/api/v1/catalog/azurelinux/busybox/details?reg=mar","page":"https://mcr.microsoft.com/en-us/product/azurelinux/busybox/about"},"readme":"## About\n\nThis repository contains the official busybox image for Azure Linux. Azure Linux busybox container is lightweight and provides a minimal attack surface.\n\nBusyBox combines tiny versions of many common UNIX utilities into a single small executable. It provides replacements for most of the utilities you usually find in GNU fileutils, shellutils, etc. The utilities in BusyBox generally have fewer options than their full-featured GNU cousins; however, the options that are included provide the expected functionality and behave very much like their GNU counterparts. BusyBox provides a fairly complete environment for any small or embedded system.\n\n### What is Azure Linux?\n\nAzure Linux is a Linux distribution for Microsoft’s cloud infrastructure and edge products and services. Azure Linux is designed to provide a consistent platform for these devices and services and will enhance Microsoft’s ability to stay current on Linux updates.  \n\nTo learn more about Azure Linux, refer to our [GitHub repository](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/).\n\n### What is a busybox container?\n\nBusybox container has only 3 Azure Linux packages i.e., filesystem, glibc, and busybox. BusyBox packages together multiple, common UNIX utilities (or applets) into one executable binary. It helps you create your own Linux distribution, and our associated container image helps you deploy it across different devices.\n\nDue to the limited set of packages, busybox container has a minimized security attack surface as well as reduced noise from vulnerability scanners. This generally translates to a reduced overhead of patching vulnerabilities, allowing developers to focus on building their application. Lastly, the smaller size (~10MB) provides higher performance.\n\n## Featured tags\n\n- `1.36`\n\nThe featured tags are multi-architecture tags (i.e., `x86-64` and `aarch64`) that refer to the latest version of their corresponding images. Additional tags that are specific to Azure Linux build or architecture can be found in the Tags section.\n\n## Usage\n\n### Run BusyBox shell\n\nFor example,\n\n    docker run -it --rm mcr.microsoft.com/azurelinux/busybox:1.36\n\nThis will drop you into an `sh` shell to allow you to do what you want inside a BusyBox system.\n\n### Create a `Dockerfile` for a binary\n   \n    FROM mcr.microsoft.com/azurelinux/busybox:1.36\n    COPY ./my-static-binary /my-static-binary\n    CMD [\"/my-static-binary\"]\n\nThis Dockerfile will allow you to create a minimal image for your statically compiled binary. You will have to compile the binary in some other place like another container or use multi-stage build.\n\n## Support\n\nAzure Linux container images are supported only for internal Microsoft use. For more information please see our [Support Statement](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/blob/-/SUPPORT.md).\n\n## License\n\n- Legal Notice: [Container License Information](https://aka.ms/mcr/osslegalnotice)\n\nThe Azure Linux distribution is provided under the [MIT license](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/blob/-/LICENSE). Individual packages and components are governed by their own licenses included in the package sources available via srpm on [packages.microsoft.com](https://packages.microsoft.com/azurelinux/) or via our [GitHub repository](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/).","readmeBase":null,"publisher":{"name":"Microsoft","url":"https://mcr.microsoft.com/","official":true,"verified":true},"registered":null,"updated":"2026-09-12T02:12:26.4753523+00:00","categories":[],"keywords":[],"license":"MIT","links":[],"maintainers":[],"stats":{"pulls":null,"pullsText":"1M+","sta